1 names it as the pre-fix gate failure of test_the_evals_tool_serves_the_bytes_it_served_before. Since then the branch changed engine semantics that whole suites pin: pass^k and the census now read kernel.scoring.failed_checks with a required catalog, and a label counts as a pass^k criterion. Dominance caps a broken floor and sets aside an uncalibrated judge's improvement. MCP structured content now carries report_read's body. The held-out writer cases were re-minted, the Kairo acceptance run re-recorded and the hot-path goldens regenerated. HEAD's own message says CI's unit shards were red, which is what hid the fastmcp-3 failure. This review is the point before the develop merge, so the full run is owed now. Run ./scripts/test.sh with no argument, so evals is included, and ./scripts/test-integration.sh -rs on 3ed12a311. Record the evidence, and account for every skip before the bundle lands.", "files": [".prawduct/.test-evidence.json", "packages/evals/tests/test_fastmcp_transport.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "A claim id is matched with only a word-character boundary, so an id inside a longer hyphenated word is rewritten", "recommendation": "instance: doc_page/build.py _claim_id builds `(?<!\\w)(?P<id>...)(?!\\w)`. A hyphen is not a word character, so a document whose claims include `c-cost` also rewrites the start of `c-cost-share`, or the tail of `x-c-cost`, in an editor's finding. The finding would then print 'claim 2-share'. Sorting longest first protects only ids that the document itself declares. Widen both lookarounds to `[\\w-]`. One new case in test_finding_names_claims_by_place.py would pin it.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/doc_page/build.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "figure_keys.py has become the report's domain layer: 8,210 lines, ~435 top-level definitions, 55 exported names and 35 importing src modules, holding the key grammar together with every rule a key reads (price frontier, lever detection, separation/settle, reader names, the grammar's reference text)", "recommendation": "class. Why it broke: 'one owner per rule' was satisfied by putting each new rule in the module that first needed it, so the grammar module absorbed the domain. Its own '# ---' section lines already mark the seams: vocabulary/matching (410), family reads (807), derived values (1846), prices and the price frontier (2071-2917), counts/examples/overlap/confusion/calls/cost gaps (2917-3341), reader names (3504), separation and settling (3834-5468, ~1,600 lines), lever effects (5468-6666, ~1,200 lines), and the grammar reference text (7662-8154). The analyst tools import their domain rules out of it (analyst/price.py: price_frontier, gain_axis, cost_basis; analyst/levers.py: arm_settings, lever_of; analyst/settle.py and compare.py: separation_wheres, assumable, pairing_contrast), as do report_decision, report_rows, report_gates, report_arms, forms and parts/look, so a change to any one rule recompiles the dependency surface of the whole report stack, and a reviewer cannot hold the module. The accepted one-owner findings (R-5, R-10, R-11) are second owners written beside rules that were hard to find in it. Construction: move each section that is a rule rather than grammar (price/frontier, separation/settle, levers, reader names, the reference text) into its own module that both figure_keys and the analyst tools import, leaving figure_keys as grammar, matcher and per-family dispatch. Bound the class: report modules added in this span past ~2,000 lines (forms.py 4,899, build.py 4,250, chart_svg.py 2,103) read against the same test; expect forms.py to split the same way (a ruled cell, a chart's texts, money).", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/figure_keys.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/price.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/levers.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/settle.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/analyst/compare.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/forms.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The analysis/report/ package and the flat analysis/report_*.py modules import each other in both directions, so the package line marks no layer, and report/decision.py (floors) and report_decision.py (caveats and dominance) share one name", "recommendation": "class. Observed chains: report_doc_store -> report.doc_page.build -> report_decision -> report.figure_keys; report.parts.{charting,resolve_*,full_results} -> report_rows / report_gates -> report.figure_keys / report.forms; report.block_spec -> report_decision. Of the eleven report_*.py modules only report_input and report_margins import nothing from report/, so 'flat means below the package' holds for those two alone, and a maintainer adding a report module has no rule for which side it goes on or what it may import; a cycle stays avoided by the current import order rather than by structure. Either move the nine that import report/ into the package (a report/doc/ for the report_doc_* set, report/rows.py, report/gates.py, report/arms.py, and report_decision beside or merged with report/decision.py under names that say floors vs caveats), keeping report_input/report_margins below it, or state the layering in report/__init__.py and pin it with an import-direction test.", "files": ["packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_decision.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/decision.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_doc_store.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_rows.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report_gates.py", "packages/evals/src/threetears/evals/analysis/report/doc_page/build.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "sev